From: hurrhnn <hurrhnn@icmp.kr>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 20:30:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] netfilter: flowtable: resolve a deferred XFRM input tuple

nft_flow_route() defers the input state of the reply tuple when a flow
that a local host opened through a packet offload tunnel is created
from a packet of that host: the original direction is plaintext from
the LAN, and the packet does not carry the state that will authenticate
the reply. nft_flow_offload_eval() then leaves NF_FLOW_HW_BIDIRECTIONAL
clear, and only the original direction is offloaded. From then on
IPS_OFFLOAD_BIT keeps every packet of the connection away from
nft_flow_route(), so nothing gives the reply tuple its state, and the
reply direction stays on the CPU until the flow ends. This happens to
every TCP connection that a local host opens, as conntrack has it
established and assured only at the ACK with which that host completes
the handshake, and to a UDP flow whose LAN side sends twice before the
first reply.

When a packet of the reply direction that nft_flow_offload_skip()
admitted with a secpath, that is one verified and policy checked
inbound packet offload state with the current policy generation and a
hardware flow id, finds IPS_OFFLOAD_BIT set, look its flow up. If the
reply tuple is still deferred, give it the state and the flow id of the
packet, set NF_FLOW_HW_BIDIRECTIONAL and queue a FLOW_CLS_REPLACE. The
flow then looks like one that an authenticated reply created.

The key is built from the conntrack tuple, as flow_offload_fill_dir()
builds it, and the input device of the packet. The packet cannot serve
as the key in the forward chain, as it does in the ingress hook, because
NAT has rewritten it by then. A deferred tuple has no encapsulation,
and its input device is the device of the XFRM route, which the packet
came in on. A packet that came in elsewhere finds no flow and changes
nothing. A state that is no longer valid is not attached. A flow whose
policy generation is no longer the current one is torn down, as the
software path does; a packet that was checked against an older
generation than the flow is ignored.

The tuple is changed with NF_FLOW_HW_PENDING owned by the work item of
the FLOW_CLS_REPLACE, which is allocated first and clears the bit once
it has run. Until then no other offload work of the flow reads the
tuple, a concurrent resolver finds the bit taken, and the GC cannot
queue the FLOW_CLS_DESTROY that has to complete before it frees the
flow. A resolver that finds the bit taken changes nothing, and the next
packet tries again. flow_offload_refresh() cannot be used: its
FLOW_CLS_REPLACE is dropped when the bit is taken, by the stats work of
the GC for example, and nothing would offer the reply direction again.
Once it owns the bit, the resolver checks that the flow is not torn
down, closing or dying and that the tuple is still deferred without a
state. These flags share the word of the bit, so a teardown that came
first is seen.

The state is held before it is published, after its flow id, with a
release store, and flow_offload_route_release() puts it when the flow
is freed, as for a state that nft_flow_route() attached. A full barrier
orders it before NF_FLOW_HW_BIDIRECTIONAL. xfrm_input_deferred stays
set: the software path hands a tuple to the slow path while either
field is set, and a lockless reader that loaded xfrm_input before the
store could otherwise load the flag after a clear and forward a packet
of the reply direction in software, without the policy check. The GC
reads xfrm_input once and sees either no state or a held one.

The FLOW_CLS_REPLACE offers both directions again. A driver that holds
the original direction refuses the second offer for its cookie, as on
every flow_offload_refresh() and for every block callback after the
first: airoha_ppe_flow_offload_replace() returns -EEXIST before it looks
at the rule, and flow_offload_rule_add() does not count that. The reply
direction is offered for the first time. If the driver refuses it, the
reply direction keeps the CPU path, as for a flow that an authenticated
reply created, and the stats and destroy requests for its cookie find no
entry.

The lookup costs one rhashtable lookup per packet of the reply
direction that carries an admitted secpath and belongs to an offloaded
connection on a hardware flowtable, which are the packets of the inbound
direction that the CPU forwards. A deferred tuple of a software
flowtable is left alone, as the software path treats both kinds alike.

A failed offer of the reply direction is not retried, as for a flow
that an authenticated reply created: the software path does not
refresh a tuple with XFRM input metadata. Refreshing there would retry
it, but it would also queue a FLOW_CLS_REPLACE every second for every
inbound flow that the driver does not take, and extend flows by packets
that have not passed the policy check yet.

A packet of the reply direction of an offloaded connection that has
already moved past ESTABLISHED, such as one whose remote host shut down
its sending side before the tuple was resolved, still reaches the
resolver; a connection that is not established gets no flow.

Signed-off-by: hurrhnn <hurrhnn@icmp.kr>
---
diff -urNp a/include/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table.h b/include/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table.h
--- a/include/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table.h
+++ b/include/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table.h
@@ -367,6 +367,10 @@ void nf_flow_offload_del(struct nf_flowt
 			 struct flow_offload *flow);
 void nf_flow_offload_stats(struct nf_flowtable *flowtable,
 			   struct flow_offload *flow);
+int nf_flow_offload_xfrm_input_resolve(struct nf_flowtable *flowtable,
+				       struct flow_offload *flow,
+				       enum flow_offload_tuple_dir dir,
+				       struct xfrm_state *x, u32 flow_id);
 
 void nf_flow_table_offload_flush(struct nf_flowtable *flowtable);
 void nf_flow_table_offload_flush_cleanup(struct nf_flowtable *flowtable);
diff -urNp a/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_offload.c b/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_offload.c
--- a/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_offload.c
+++ b/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_offload.c
@@ -1240,6 +1240,77 @@ void nf_flow_offload_add(struct nf_flowt
 	flow_offload_queue_work(offload);
 }
 
+/**
+ * nf_flow_offload_xfrm_input_resolve - give a deferred tuple its input state
+ * @flowtable: flowtable that holds @flow
+ * @flow: hardware offloaded flow
+ * @dir: direction whose tuple deferred its XFRM input state
+ * @x: authenticated input state of a packet of @dir, held by the caller
+ * @flow_id: hardware flow id of that packet
+ *
+ * Attach @x and @flow_id to the deferred tuple, offload both directions
+ * from now on and queue a FLOW_CLS_REPLACE for them.
+ *
+ * Return: 0 on success, -EBUSY if other offload work of @flow is in flight
+ * or no memory is left for the work (a later packet may try again), or
+ * another negative error code if the tuple cannot or need not be resolved.
+ */
+int nf_flow_offload_xfrm_input_resolve(struct nf_flowtable *flowtable,
+				       struct flow_offload *flow,
+				       enum flow_offload_tuple_dir dir,
+				       struct xfrm_state *x, u32 flow_id)
+{
+	struct flow_offload_tuple *tuple = &flow->tuplehash[dir].tuple;
+	struct flow_offload_work *offload;
+
+	if (!nf_flowtable_hw_offload(flowtable) ||
+	    !test_bit(NF_FLOW_HW, &flow->flags) ||
+	    tuple->xmit_type == FLOW_OFFLOAD_XMIT_XFRM)
+		return -EOPNOTSUPP;
+
+	/* Own NF_FLOW_HW_PENDING before the tuple changes. Until the work
+	 * queued below has run, no other offload work of the flow reads the
+	 * tuple, no other resolver writes it, and the GC cannot queue the
+	 * FLOW_CLS_DESTROY that has to complete before it frees the flow.
+	 */
+	offload = nf_flow_offload_work_alloc(flowtable, flow, FLOW_CLS_REPLACE);
+	if (!offload)
+		return -EBUSY;
+
+	if (test_bit(NF_FLOW_TEARDOWN, &flow->flags) ||
+	    test_bit(NF_FLOW_CLOSING, &flow->flags) ||
+	    test_bit(NF_FLOW_HW_DYING, &flow->flags) ||
+	    !tuple->xfrm_input_deferred || tuple->xfrm_input) {
+		clear_bit(NF_FLOW_HW_PENDING, &flow->flags);
+		kfree(offload);
+		return -EALREADY;
+	}
+
+	/* The software path sends a tuple to the slow path while either
+	 * xfrm_input or xfrm_input_deferred is set. Keep the flag, so that a
+	 * lockless reader never sees both clear.
+	 */
+	xfrm_state_hold(x);
+	tuple->xfrm_input_flow_id = flow_id;
+	/* Publish the state after its flow id. */
+	smp_store_release(&tuple->xfrm_input, x);
+	/* Order the state before the flag, and so before the work below and
+	 * the GC pass that later frees the flow and puts the state.
+	 */
+	smp_mb__before_atomic();
+	set_bit(NF_FLOW_HW_BIDIRECTIONAL, &flow->flags);
+	WRITE_ONCE(flow->timeout,
+		   nf_flowtable_time_stamp + flow_offload_get_timeout(flow));
+
+	/* The other direction is offloaded again as well, which a driver
+	 * already holding it refuses, as on every flow_offload_refresh().
+	 */
+	flow_offload_queue_work(offload);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(nf_flow_offload_xfrm_input_resolve);
+
 void nf_flow_offload_del(struct nf_flowtable *flowtable,
 			 struct flow_offload *flow)
 {
diff -urNp a/net/netfilter/nft_flow_offload.c b/net/netfilter/nft_flow_offload.c
--- a/net/netfilter/nft_flow_offload.c
+++ b/net/netfilter/nft_flow_offload.c
@@ -160,6 +160,104 @@ static bool nft_flow_offload_bridge_ok(c
 	return true;
 }
 
+/* A flow that a local host opens through a packet offload tunnel defers the
+ * input state of its reply tuple when a packet of that host creates it, see
+ * nft_flow_route(), and is offloaded in the original direction only.
+ * Give the deferred tuple the state of the first authenticated packet of the
+ * reply direction that reaches this expression, so that the reply direction
+ * is offloaded as well.
+ */
+static void nft_flow_offload_xfrm_resolve(const struct nft_pktinfo *pkt,
+					  struct nf_flowtable *flowtable,
+					  struct nf_conn *ct)
+{
+#if IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_XFRM)
+	const struct nf_conntrack_tuple *ctt =
+		&ct->tuplehash[IP_CT_DIR_REPLY].tuple;
+	struct flow_offload_tuple_rhash *tuplehash;
+	const struct net_device *in = nft_in(pkt);
+	struct flow_offload_tuple tuple = {};
+	struct xfrm_offload *xo;
+	struct flow_offload *flow;
+	struct sec_path *sp;
+	struct xfrm_state *x;
+	u32 genid;
+
+	if (!in)
+		return;
+
+	/* nft_flow_offload_skip() admits a packet with a secpath only for one
+	 * verified, policy checked inbound packet offload state, with the
+	 * current policy generation and a hardware flow id.
+	 */
+	sp = skb_sec_path(pkt->skb);
+	if (!sp || !nf_flowtable_hw_offload(flowtable))
+		return;
+
+	xo = xfrm_input_offload(pkt->skb);
+	if (!xo)
+		return;
+
+	x = sp->xvec[0];
+	if (READ_ONCE(x->km.state) != XFRM_STATE_VALID)
+		return;
+
+	/* Build the key from the conntrack tuple, as flow_offload_fill_dir()
+	 * does: NAT has already changed the packet in this hook. A deferred
+	 * tuple has no encapsulation, and its input device is the device of
+	 * the XFRM route, which the packet came in on.
+	 */
+	switch (ctt->src.l3num) {
+	case NFPROTO_IPV4:
+		tuple.src_v4 = ctt->src.u3.in;
+		tuple.dst_v4 = ctt->dst.u3.in;
+		break;
+	case NFPROTO_IPV6:
+		tuple.src_v6 = ctt->src.u3.in6;
+		tuple.dst_v6 = ctt->dst.u3.in6;
+		break;
+	default:
+		return;
+	}
+	tuple.l3proto = ctt->src.l3num;
+	tuple.l4proto = ctt->dst.protonum;
+	switch (ctt->dst.protonum) {
+	case IPPROTO_TCP:
+	case IPPROTO_UDP:
+		tuple.src_port = ctt->src.u.tcp.port;
+		tuple.dst_port = ctt->dst.u.tcp.port;
+		break;
+	}
+	tuple.iifidx = in->ifindex;
+
+	tuplehash = flow_offload_lookup(flowtable, &tuple);
+	if (!tuplehash || tuplehash->tuple.dir != FLOW_OFFLOAD_DIR_REPLY ||
+	    !tuplehash->tuple.xfrm_input_deferred ||
+	    READ_ONCE(tuplehash->tuple.xfrm_input))
+		return;
+
+	flow = container_of(tuplehash, struct flow_offload,
+			    tuplehash[FLOW_OFFLOAD_DIR_REPLY]);
+	if (flow->ct != ct)
+		return;
+
+	/* A flow from before a policy change is torn down, as the software
+	 * path would do; a packet checked before the change is ignored.
+	 */
+	genid = tuplehash->tuple.xfrm_policy_genid;
+	if (genid != xfrm_policy_generation(nft_net(pkt))) {
+		flow_offload_teardown(flow);
+		return;
+	}
+	if (genid != sp->policy_genid)
+		return;
+
+	nf_flow_offload_xfrm_input_resolve(flowtable, flow,
+					   FLOW_OFFLOAD_DIR_REPLY, x,
+					   xo->hw_flow_id);
+#endif
+}
+
 static void nft_flow_offload_eval(const struct nft_expr *expr,
 				  struct nft_regs *regs,
 				  const struct nft_pktinfo *pkt)
@@ -194,8 +292,17 @@ static void nft_flow_offload_eval(const
 		if (unlikely(tcph->fin || tcph->rst ||
 			     (tcph->syn && !tcph->ack)))
 			goto out;
-		if (unlikely(!nf_conntrack_tcp_established(ct)))
+		if (unlikely(!nf_conntrack_tcp_established(ct))) {
+			u8 state = READ_ONCE(ct->proto.tcp.state);
+
+			if (routing && CTINFO2DIR(ctinfo) == IP_CT_DIR_REPLY &&
+			    state >= TCP_CONNTRACK_FIN_WAIT &&
+			    state <= TCP_CONNTRACK_TIME_WAIT &&
+			    test_bit(IPS_OFFLOAD_BIT, &ct->status))
+				nft_flow_offload_xfrm_resolve(pkt, flowtable,
+							      ct);
 			goto out;
+		}
 		break;
 	case IPPROTO_UDP:
 		break;
@@ -226,8 +333,12 @@ static void nft_flow_offload_eval(const
 	if (!routing && !nft_flow_offload_bridge_ok(ct))
 		goto out;
 
-	if (test_and_set_bit(IPS_OFFLOAD_BIT, &ct->status))
+	if (test_and_set_bit(IPS_OFFLOAD_BIT, &ct->status)) {
+		/* nft_flow_route() defers only the reply tuple. */
+		if (routing && CTINFO2DIR(ctinfo) == IP_CT_DIR_REPLY)
+			nft_flow_offload_xfrm_resolve(pkt, flowtable, ct);
 		goto out;
+	}
 
 	dir = CTINFO2DIR(ctinfo);
 	if (nft_flow_route(pkt, ct, &route, dir, priv->flowtable) < 0)
